Creating a custom WordPress block is a great way to extend the functionality of the Gutenberg editor.

The modern standard for creating blocks is using the **React** library with the **@wordpress/block-editor** package.

Here is a step-by-step guide to creating a block that contains a Heading, Paragraph, and Image.

### Prerequisites
1.  **Node.js** installed on your computer.
2.  A local WordPress installation (or a local environment like LocalWP).
3.  Basic knowledge of JavaScript/React.

---

### Step 1: Create the Project
Open your terminal and create a new directory for your block.

```bash
mkdir my-custom-block
cd my-custom-block
npm init -y
```

### Step 2: Install Dependencies
You need the WordPress block editor packages and React.

```bash
npm install @wordpress/block-editor @wordpress/blocks @wordpress/components @wordpress/element @wordpress/i18n react react-dom
```

### Step 3: Create the Block File
Create a file named `index.js` in your project folder. This file will contain the logic for your block.

```javascript
/**
 * "My Custom Block"
 */
import { registerBlockType } from '@wordpress/blocks';
import { RichText, MediaUpload, MediaUploadCheck } from '@wordpress/block-editor';
import { InspectorControls } from '@wordpress/block-editor';
import { PanelBody, TextControl } from '@wordpress/components';
import { useState } from '@wordpress/element';

const edit = (props) => {
  const { attributes, setAttributes } = props;
  const [heading, setHeading] = useState(attributes.heading || '');
  const [content, setContent] = useState(attributes.content || '');
  const [imageId, setImageId] = useState(attributes.imageId || null);

  const updateHeading = (newHeading) => {
    setHeading(newHeading);
    setAttributes({ heading: newHeading });
  };

  const updateContent = (newContent) => {
    setContent(newContent);
    setAttributes({ content: newContent });
  };

  const updateImage = (newImageId) => {
    setImageId(newImageId);
    setAttributes({ imageId: newImageId });
  };

  return (
    <>
      <InspectorControls>
        <PanelBody title="Block Settings">
          <TextControl
            label="Heading"
            value={heading}
            onChange={updateHeading}
          />
        </PanelBody>
      </InspectorControls>

      <div className="custom-block-wrapper">
        {heading && <h2>{heading}</h2>}
        {content && <p>{content}</p>}

        {imageId && (
          <MediaUploadCheck>
            <img 
              src={wp.media.getMediaUrl(imageId)} 
              alt="Block Image" 
              style={{ maxWidth: '100%', height: 'auto' }} 
            />
          </MediaUploadCheck>
        )}

        <MediaUpload
          onSelect={updateImage}
          render={({ open }) => (
            <button onClick={open} style={{ marginTop: '10px' }}>
              {imageId ? 'Change Image' : 'Add Image'}
            </button>
          )}
          value={imageId}
          type="image"
        />
      </div>
    </>
  );
};

